;; The kernel's side of processes on amd64 (doc/go/PROCESS-SPEC.md §2.2, §3.2, KU1): the vCPU
;; prepared for ring 3 (UserSetup), a process entered and left (EnterUser), its faults told from
;; the kernel's own (boot_amd64.s's vector_common). Nothing here runs unless the kernel calls
;; UserSetup: the box's guest is as it was.
;;
;; One image is the kernel and every process (b1): a process's data and bss are at the kernel's
;; addresses for them, in its own pages. So the kernel's globals are not the kernel's while a
;; process's tables are loaded, and every crossing writes CR3: into the process's tables on the
;; way in, back into the kernel's on the way out (PROCESS-SPEC.md, "As built: KU1"). What the
;; crossing touches meanwhile is all outside the image's data: the per-vCPU area (userCPU, named
;; by GS), the context, the GDT, the kernel's stack, all on the kernel's heap.
